Subject: One facts_valid word per compiled region: 64-bit dependents on fact groups and layouts, METHOD / PROTO-IDENTITY / ABSENT helpers, one funnel for layout breaks

What this does
  - `qjs_aot_fact_depend64(g, word, mask)`: a bit of a uint64_t the caller owns depends on a fact group. `qjs_aot_layout_depend(ctx, layout, word, mask)`: a bit depends on a contracted layout (1..63). Neither arms the bit. `qjs_aot_facts_commit(ctx, word, mask)` sets the bits only when every dependent registered on them is VALID / unbroken, so a bit is the AND of its dependents (the 32-bit `qjs_aot_fact_depend` set its bit as soon as ONE group was valid, which is wrong for two groups sharing a bit; it is kept as it was). Any break clears the bits of its dependents; `qjs_aot_facts_undepend(ctx, word)` forgets a word; `JS_FreeRuntime` clears every bit it ever armed, since the words live in the unit's statics and outlive the runtime.
  - Generated code tests `QJS_AOT_FACTS_OK(word, MASK)` (quickjs.h): one volatile 64-bit load and a compare.
  - Layouts are folded in: every layout break already passes `js_aot_layout_break` (leaving the layout's shape, an instance becoming a prototype, a refused slot claim, a refused claim at bind); that function now also clears the layout's dependents, next to the old `aot_layout_broken` bit, which stays as the mirror that generated code and the claim tables read. There is no second place that decides a layout is broken.
  - Group kinds the region compiler needs: METHOD `qjs_aot_fact_method(ctx, holder, atom, fn, digest)` (own data property `atom` of `holder` is `fn`, optionally with the bytecode digest of `fn` checked), PROTO-IDENTITY `qjs_aot_fact_proto_is(ctx, obj, proto)`, and ABSENT `qjs_aot_fact_absent(ctx, g, atom)` (no own property `atom`; adding it breaks the group; CLOSED counts present properties only). A method found two prototypes up is `absent(P, atom) + proto_is(P, Q) + method(Q, atom, fn)`.
  - `qjs_aot_layouts_broken(rt)` reports the broken layout bits. `QJS_AOT_FACTS_OFF=layout` (kit knob builds) stops layout breaks from clearing facts_valid bits, for a negative control.

Why we need it
  The region compiler guards a whole fast body with one `facts_valid` word instead of a test per fact (AOT-REDESIGN.md section 4/5). Today a layout is tested through the runtime-wide `aot_layout_broken` word and a per-function `claimdeps` mask; groups are tested not at all. One word per region instance, fed by both, is the single thing generated code reads.

Soundness
  Breaks only clear bits, synchronously, at or before the mutation they report; nothing here blocks, allocates, frees or calls JS. A region must test the word at entry, after an interrupt-poll slow path that ran a handler, and after any call with unknown effects (a call, a getter/setter/Proxy trap, a native method, a release that can run a host finalizer, a host callback), because a break can clear a bit inside any of them; it must not cache the word across such a point. A bit that a group makes invalid before `qjs_aot_facts_commit` is never armed. `qjs_aot_fact_depend64` of a group that is not VALID clears the bit. The eval scan still breaks more groups than the mutators do (any code that stores a listed atom, even to another object).

Functions touched
  `js_aot_layout_break`, `js_aot_fact_matches`, `js_aot_fact_set_deps`, `js_aot_facts_free`, `js_aot_facts_off_mask`, `struct JSAotFactDep` / `JSAotFacts`, and new: `qjs_aot_fact_depend64`, `qjs_aot_layout_depend`, `qjs_aot_facts_commit`, `qjs_aot_facts_undepend`, `qjs_aot_layouts_broken`, `qjs_aot_fact_absent`, `qjs_aot_fact_method`, `qjs_aot_fact_proto_is`, `js_aot_layout_facts_break`.

No.

